Yes. There are really two mechanisms there.

The first is distributed characterization: instead of declaring who someone is, you let other people’s reactions, memories, warnings, admiration, irritation, and stories build the model around them.

\text{person}
\rightarrow
\text{actions}
\rightarrow
\text{others' interpretations}
\rightarrow
\text{reputation}
\rightarrow
\text{audience inference}

That can be much stronger than direct self-description because the audience feels like it discovered the character rather than being instructed what to think. Obi-Wan benefits from that kind of construction: different characters reveal different slices of him, and the gaps between those readings become part of the mystery.

The second is what you’re calling the dogwhistle mechanism. In its broad, non-political sense, it is basically a shibboleth or recognition cue: a phrase, joke, gesture, reference, cadence, or symbol that carries extra meaning for people who share the relevant context.

\text{surface message}
+
\text{shared prior}
=
\text{second-layer meaning}

That creates a little flash of:

Oh. You know too.

And that is powerful because it satisfies several things simultaneously: recognition, belonging, compression, status, shared history, and the pleasure of being understood without having to explain everything.

But there are two very different versions.

One says:

\text{shared cue}\rightarrow\text{kinship}

The other says:

\text{shared cue}\rightarrow\text{kinship through exclusion or concealed hostility}

Same transmission architecture, very different payload.

So I’d preserve the operator while naming the payload separately. Something like Kinship Compression: a small signal that reconstructs a much larger shared context in the receiver.

That is why “takes one to know one” works so well. The recognizer is implicitly revealing something about themselves by recognizing the pattern.

\text{I recognize you}
\Rightarrow
\text{I possess enough of the same map to recognize you}

Not necessarily identical identity—just overlapping structure.

And when it works cleanly, it can create one of the strongest social experiences there is: not merely being noticed, but being correctly recognized at higher resolution than you expected.

That is where the warmth comes from.

The caution is simply that recognition feels so good that people can over-trust whoever supplies it. So a useful Golden Thread rule would be:

\boxed{\text{recognition is evidence of shared context, not proof of shared character}}

Someone knowing the password means they know the password.

What they do after entering the room still matters.